  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: "Alex Rivers",
    section: "Home Improvement",
    tags: ["lastiseal", "brick sealer", "concrete sealer", "penetrating sealer", "masonry", "radonseal"],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{ text: "Jump to Our Verdict", link: "#verdict" }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div
        class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10"
        style="background-image: url('data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg width=\'60\' height=\'60\' viewBox=\'0 0 60 60\' xmlns=\'http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\'%3E%3Cg fill=\'none\' fill-rule=\'evenodd\'%3E%3Cg fill=\'%23ffffff\' fill-opacity=\'1\'%3E%3Cpath d=\'M36 34v-4h-2v4h-4v2h4v4h2v-4h4v-2h-4zm0-30V0h-2v4h-4v2h4v4h2V6h4V4h-4zM6 34v-4H4v4H0v2h4v4h2v-4h4v-2H6zM6 4V0H4v4H0v2h4v4h2V6h4V4H6z\'/%3E%3C/g%3E%3C/g%3E%3C/svg%3E');"
      ></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6"><TrustBadge /></div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">LastiSeal</span> Review: Does the 15-Year Claim Hold Up?
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          A penetrating sealer that polymerises inside the masonry rather than forming a film on top of it. The chemistry is genuinely different from most of what is on the shelf — and so is the amount of preparation it demands before it will do anything at all.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a href="#verdict" class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline">Skip to The Verdict</a>
          <a href="#how-it-works" class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline">How It Works</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="4.9k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#1E90FF" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><line x1="8" y1="6" x2="21" y2="6"></line><line x1="8" y1="12" x2="21" y2="12"></line><line x1="8" y1="18" x2="21" y2="18"></line><line x1="3" y1="6" x2="3.01" y2="6"></line><line x1="3" y1="12" x2="3.01" y2="12"></line><line x1="3" y1="18" x2="3.01" y2="18"></line></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#what-it-is" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. What LastiSeal Is</a>
          <a href="#how-it-works" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. How the Chemistry Works</a>
          <a href="#the-lineup" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. The Product Lineup</a>
          <a href="#vs-alternatives" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. LastiSeal vs the Alternatives</a>
          <a href="#application" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Applying It Without Wasting It</a>
          <a href="#who-its-not-for" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Who It Is Not For</a>
          <a href="#common-mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-16 lg:py-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic border-l-4 border-[#38BDF8] pl-6 bg-gray-50 py-4 rounded-r-xl">
          Most masonry sealer complaints come down to one failure: a film applied to a surface that later lets go of it. LastiSeal's entire pitch is that there is no film to let go. Whether that pitch survives contact with your particular wall depends almost entirely on how porous it is and how thoroughly you clean it.
        </p>

        <h2 id="what-it-is" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          1. What LastiSeal Is
        </h2>

        <p>
          LastiSeal is a water-based penetrating sealer sold by RadonSeal for brick, concrete, concrete block, stucco and other porous masonry. It is not a coating in the ordinary sense: applied to open masonry it soaks in, and the polymer it carries cures inside the pore structure rather than on top of it. When the job is finished there is nothing on the surface to see, touch, or eventually peel.
        </p>

        <p>
          That places it in the same broad family as the penetrating sealers we cover in our <a href="/best-brick-sealer-reviews/">brick sealer reviews</a> and <a href="/best-cinder-block-sealer-reviews/">cinder block sealer guide</a>, but with a distinct mechanism. Silane-siloxane products line the pores with a water-repellent layer while leaving them open; LastiSeal fills and polymerises within them. The result is a more complete block against liquid water and a corresponding reduction in how freely the masonry breathes — a trade-off that matters, and one we come back to below.
        </p>

        <p>
          The headline commercial claim is a 15-year warranty on properly applied product. That is unusual in a category where two to four years is normal for film-forming sealers, and the mechanism does support it: a polymer locked below the surface is not exposed to ultraviolet light, foot traffic, or abrasion, which are the three things that destroy surface films.
        </p>

        <h2 id="how-it-works" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          2. How the Chemistry Works
        </h2>

        <p>
          Porous masonry is a network of interconnected capillaries. Water travels through them by capillary action, carrying dissolved salts, and in cold climates freezes inside them — which is what spalls brick faces and crumbles mortar over decades. Anything that stops water entering those capillaries addresses the root cause rather than the appearance.
        </p>

        <p>
          LastiSeal is thin enough to be drawn into that capillary network by the same forces that draw water in. Once inside, the carrier evaporates and the polymer chains link together, forming a solid that is physically locked inside the pores. Because the polymer is inside the material rather than bonded to its face, there is no adhesion plane for water pressure or freeze-thaw movement to break. This is the same argument that makes penetrating sealers the right answer on <a href="/sealing-old-concrete/">old concrete</a>, where a film has too many contaminants to hold on to.
        </p>

        <p>
          There is a secondary effect worth knowing about: on friable, deteriorating masonry, filling the pore structure with cured polymer also consolidates the surface slightly, firming up material that was starting to dust. It is not a structural repair and should not be mistaken for one, but on soft old brick it is a genuine benefit that a surface film cannot offer.
        </p>

        <p>
          The limitation follows directly from the mechanism. The product must get in. On dense, power-troweled concrete, on glazed or engineering brick, or on any surface still carrying a previous sealer, penetration is limited or nil — and a penetrating sealer that cannot penetrate does nothing whatsoever.
        </p>

        <div class="my-16 bg-gradient-to-br from-gray-950 to-gray-900 rounded-3xl p-8 lg:p-12 text-white shadow-2xl">
          <h2 id="what-it-does" class="scroll-mt-24 text-2xl font-black text-white mt-0 mb-6 border-none">What LastiSeal Does — and Doesn't Do</h2>
          <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-8 text-gray-300">
            <div>
              <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-green-400 mb-3">It Does</p>
              <ul class="space-y-2 text-sm">
                <li>Stop porous brick and block absorbing liquid water</li>
                <li>Slow freeze-thaw spalling and efflorescence</li>
                <li>Cure below the surface, so nothing can peel</li>
                <li>Leave appearance and slip resistance unchanged</li>
                <li>Consolidate soft, dusting masonry slightly</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
            <div>
              <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#FF4500] mb-3">It Doesn't</p>
              <ul class="space-y-2 text-sm">
                <li>Penetrate dense or previously sealed surfaces</li>
                <li>Produce a wet look or colour enhancement</li>
                <li>Bridge cracks or repair spalled masonry</li>
                <li>Replace a pressure-rated basement waterproofer</li>
                <li>Forgive poor cleaning — at all</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h2 id="the-lineup" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          3. The Product Lineup
        </h2>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">LastiSeal Brick &amp; Masonry Sealer</h3>
        <p>
          The core product and the one most people mean by "LastiSeal". Clear, water-based, intended for brick, block, stucco and porous concrete. On a porous brick wall it is genuinely impressive: water that previously soaked straight in sits on the surface and runs off, with no visible change to the brick itself. This is the version to buy for a house wall, a chimney, or a porous garden wall.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">LastiSeal Concrete Stain &amp; Sealer</h3>
        <p>
          A hybrid that carries pigment into the pores along with the polymer, colouring and sealing in one operation. It suits faded stamped or coloured concrete where a topcoat would look plasticky, and we cover it in more detail in our <a href="/best-stamped-concrete-sealer/">stamped concrete sealer guide</a>. It demands the same immaculate preparation as the clear version, with the added risk that any contamination shows up as uneven colour rather than merely as uneven protection.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">RadonSeal Deep Penetrating Concrete Sealer</h3>
        <p>
          A sibling product rather than a LastiSeal variant. It is a reactive sodium silicate that crystallises inside poured concrete, and its niche is dense concrete — foundation walls and slabs — where it also reduces radon and vapour transmission. If your substrate is dense poured concrete rather than porous brick or block, this is usually the more appropriate product from the same family.
        </p>

        <h2 id="vs-alternatives" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          4. LastiSeal vs the Alternatives
        </h2>

        <p>
          <strong>Versus film-forming acrylics.</strong> No contest on longevity. Acrylics give a wet look and colour depth that penetrating sealers cannot, and they last two to four years before they need stripping and recoating. LastiSeal offers no appearance change and, applied correctly, no recoating cycle. Choose acrylic for looks, LastiSeal for permanence.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Versus silane-siloxane.</strong> This is the real comparison, and it is closer. Silane-siloxane products such as the Foundation Armor and Ghostshield sealers line pores while leaving them open, so the masonry keeps breathing — important on a wall with any moisture coming from behind. LastiSeal blocks liquid water more completely but reduces vapour transmission. For most exterior brick above grade, silane-siloxane is the safer default; for very porous or deteriorating masonry that would benefit from consolidation, LastiSeal is the stronger choice. Our <a href="/silane-vs-siloxane-sealers/">silane vs siloxane guide</a> explains that side of the choice.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Versus cementitious waterproofers.</strong> Different jobs entirely. On a basement wall dealing with hydrostatic pressure, a rated waterproofer such as DRYLOK is the correct product and LastiSeal is not a substitute. Sealing porosity is not the same as resisting pressure, and our <a href="/best-basement-waterproofing-paint/">basement waterproofing paint guide</a> covers where that line falls.
        </p>

        <h2 id="application" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          5. Applying It Without Wasting It
        </h2>

        <p>
          Almost every negative LastiSeal review we have read describes a preparation problem rather than a product problem. The application itself is easy; the conditions it requires are strict.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Test the Surface First</h3>
        <p>
          Sprinkle water on several areas. If it soaks in and darkens the masonry within about thirty seconds, the surface is open and ready. If it beads or sits, something is blocking it — an old sealer, dirt, or a dense surface — and the product will sit on top and be wasted. This thirty-second test is the single most valuable thing on this page.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Clean Thoroughly, Then Wait</h3>
        <p>
          Remove any existing sealer, efflorescence, dirt, algae and oil. Brush efflorescence off dry rather than washing it in. If you have pressure-washed, allow a full 24 to 48 hours of drying — longer in cool or humid weather — because pores full of water cannot accept sealer either.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Apply Wet-on-Wet, Thin</h3>
        <p>
          Use a pump sprayer and apply two to three thin coats, each while the previous one is still damp. That is the key technique: it keeps the capillaries open and carries the polymer deeper. A single flooded coat seals the outermost layer, leaves the depth untreated, and dries as a residue on the face.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Budget for Coverage, Not for Area</h3>
        <p>
          Coverage figures on penetrating sealers are a range rather than a number, because a thirsty old brick wall can take twice what a tighter one does. Measure the area, then buy for the top of the stated range rather than the middle — running short partway along a wall means a visible line where the treated and untreated sections meet after the first rain.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Do a Test Patch and Wait a Day</h3>
        <p>
          On brick and coloured concrete especially, treat a square yard somewhere inconspicuous and leave it 24 hours. Very porous or previously stained masonry can darken slightly, and finding that out on a test area is considerably better than finding it out across a house frontage.
        </p>

        <h2 id="who-its-not-for" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          6. Who It Is Not For
        </h2>

        <p>
          <strong>Anyone wanting a wet look.</strong> There is no gloss, no colour deepening, no enhancement of any kind from the clear product. If the goal is to make brick or concrete look richer, buy a film-forming sealer and accept the maintenance cycle.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Dense concrete.</strong> Power-troweled slabs, dense pavers and engineering brick do not have the capillary structure this product needs. The RadonSeal reactive silicate is the better match for dense poured concrete.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Anyone who will not do the prep.</strong> This is not a product that tolerates a quick sweep and a spray. A cheaper sealer applied over a dirty surface fails visibly and is easy to strip; LastiSeal applied over a dirty surface simply does not go in, and the money is gone.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Basement walls under pressure.</strong> Useful against moisture migrating through porous block, but not a substitute for a rated waterproofing system — and never a substitute for fixing drainage.
        </p>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">Common Mistakes With LastiSeal</h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Applying It Over an Old Sealer</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The most expensive mistake available. Any previous sealer blocks penetration entirely, so the product dries on the surface and does nothing. Run the water test on several areas before opening the container.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Flooding On a Single Heavy Coat</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                More product in one pass seals the outer millimetre and stops the rest getting in. Two or three thin coats wet-on-wet penetrate several times deeper and use the material better.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing Damp Masonry</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Pores already full of water cannot take up sealer. Allow a full 24 to 48 hours after cleaning or rain, and longer on a shaded north wall that never really dries.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting It to Fix Spalling or Cracks</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                It seals pores; it does not bridge gaps or rebuild faces. Repoint failed mortar and repair spalled brick first, let the repairs cure, then seal to slow the damage recurring.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the Test Patch on Coloured Masonry</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The clear product is designed not to change appearance, but very porous or stained masonry can darken slightly. One square yard and a day of patience is cheap insurance across a whole wall.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Verdict -->
        <div id="verdict" class="my-16 bg-gradient-to-br from-gray-950 to-gray-900 rounded-3xl p-8 lg:p-12 text-white shadow-2xl relative overflow-hidden">
          <div class="absolute top-0 right-0 bg-[#FF4500] text-white font-black text-xs uppercase tracking-widest px-4 py-2 rounded-bl-xl">
            ★ Our Verdict
          </div>
          <h2 id="our-verdict" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-white mt-0 mb-6 border-none">LastiSeal: Our Verdict</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6 text-gray-300">
            <p>
              <strong class="text-green-400">Buy it if:</strong> you have porous brick, block, stucco or older concrete, you want protection that does not need redoing every few years, and you are willing to strip and clean the surface properly first. On that substrate, prepared that way, it does exactly what it claims and the long warranty is credible.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong class="text-[#FF4500]">Skip it if:</strong> the masonry is dense or already sealed, you want a wet look, or you were hoping to spray it over a surface that has not been cleaned. In those cases a cheaper acrylic or a silane-siloxane will serve you better, and the premium buys nothing.
            </p>
            <p class="text-lg border-t border-gray-700 pt-6 mt-6">
              <strong class="text-white">The Honest Bottom Line:</strong> LastiSeal is a good product with an unusually narrow set of conditions. It is not more forgiving than its competitors — it is considerably less forgiving, because everything depends on penetration. Meet the conditions and you get a wall that has stopped absorbing water with no film to maintain; miss them and you have spent a premium on a surface that is exactly as it was.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <h2 id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          Frequently Asked Questions
        </h2>

        <div class="space-y-6">
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Does LastiSeal actually work?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">On properly cleaned porous masonry, yes — it penetrates and polymerises inside the pore structure, so there is no film to peel and the brick or block stops absorbing water. Its weakness is tolerance rather than chemistry: on dense, sealed or contaminated surfaces it cannot get in, and a penetrating sealer that cannot penetrate does nothing.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Is LastiSeal better than a silane-siloxane sealer?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">They work differently. Silane-siloxane lines the pores and leaves the masonry breathable; LastiSeal fills and polymerises inside them, blocking water more completely but reducing vapour transmission. Silane-siloxane is the safer default on most exterior brick; LastiSeal is stronger on very porous, deteriorating masonry that benefits from consolidation.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Does LastiSeal change the appearance of brick?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">The clear product is designed to leave appearance unchanged — no gloss, no wet look, no darkening — because nothing remains on the surface. Very porous or previously stained masonry can darken slightly, so a test patch is worth the day it costs. The Stain and Sealer version is a separate product that deliberately adds colour.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does LastiSeal last?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">The manufacturer warrants properly applied product for 15 years, and the mechanism supports a long life — polymer locked inside the pores is not exposed to UV or abrasion. The caveat is that the warranty assumes a correctly prepared, sufficiently porous substrate, which is the same condition that decides whether it works at all.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you apply LastiSeal over an existing sealer?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">No. Any existing sealer blocks penetration and the product will sit on the surface. Strip and clean back to bare, open masonry first. The water test decides it: if water beads instead of soaking in within about thirty seconds, the surface is not ready.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">How many coats do you need?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Two to three thin coats applied wet-on-wet, each going on while the previous is still damp. That technique keeps the capillaries open and carries the polymer deeper. One heavy flooded coat seals the outer layer and leaves residue on the face.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Is LastiSeal worth the price?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">On porous brick, block or old concrete that would otherwise need resealing every two to four years, one careful application beats several rounds of stripping and recoating. On dense concrete where it cannot fully penetrate, or where a wet look is the goal, cheaper products do the job better.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Can LastiSeal be used on basement walls?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">It helps against moisture migrating through porous block, but it is not a substitute for a rated waterproofing coating where hydrostatic pressure is involved. For active seepage, use a cementitious waterproofer with a published pressure rating — and fix the drainage before either.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

